1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,512
At a rally in Montana this October, President Trump
told the crowd, "we have very little inflation".

2
00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:14,565
On CBS News, a politics reporter put it the other
way: "inflation is higher than when he took office".

3
00:00:14,567 --> 00:00:20,711
Very little, or more than before? Let's put both
claims against the government's own price index.

4
00:00:21,733 --> 00:00:27,661
Start with the boldest version: Trump has
also said, flatly, "we don't have inflation".

5
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:37,723
The Bureau of Labor Statistics' September report says otherwise: consumer prices
were 3.7 percent higher than a year earlier, and still rising month to month.

6
00:00:37,733 --> 00:00:43,805
So there is inflation. The real argument is whether it's
high, and that depends on what you compare it with.

7
00:00:44,333 --> 00:00:51,461
Trump's comparison is the past: "we inherited the worst
inflation in history", he told the Montana rally.

8
00:00:51,467 --> 00:01:03,179
These figures can't rank all of history, but the 2022 peak was real: in June
2022 the yearly rate hit 9.1 percent, and today's is less than half of that.

9
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:13,852
And early this year, the low-inflation case was strong. Of January's reading, the YouTube
channel The Right-Wing Populist said it was "the lowest we've had in five years".

10
00:01:13,867 --> 00:01:25,603
Close enough: the January rate was 2.4 percent, a whisker above the lowest reading
since March 2021 and about the pace of early 2020 just before the pandemic.

11
00:01:25,633 --> 00:01:32,929
By September, that case had gone: the yearly rate
was higher than in any month of 2024 or 2025.

12
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:43,673
It was also above the 3.0 percent of January 2025 (the month the administration
took office): what he actually inherited, and the comparison CBS was making.

13
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:52,068
Trump has a shorter window in mind: "we're at 1.7%
inflation for the last three months", he told reporters.

14
00:01:52,100 --> 00:02:01,244
The index says otherwise: from June to September, the seasonally
adjusted index ran at a pace of about 4.6 percent a year.

15
00:02:01,567 --> 00:02:10,663
To be fair, slide that window back one month, to end in August, and the
pace drops to about 0.2 percent a year, because prices fell in June.

16
00:02:10,667 --> 00:02:13,211
Pick your window, and
you pick your answer.

17
00:02:14,233 --> 00:02:22,801
The Federal Reserve, for its part, said at its September press conference
that "for more than five years, inflation has been running above target".

18
00:02:22,833 --> 00:02:35,961
Its stated goal is 2 percent, and its own officials expect 3.7 percent over 2026 on the
price index they forecast, called PCE, short for personal consumption expenditures.

19
00:02:35,967 --> 00:02:44,703
On the consumer price index, the yearly rate hasn't dropped
below 2.3 percent in any month with a reading since March 2021.

20
00:02:45,233 --> 00:02:47,825
And if the rate does
come down, will prices?

21
00:02:47,833 --> 00:02:54,889
Even The Right-Wing Populist says not: "prices are
technically expanding, but the actual rate is decreasing".

22
00:02:54,900 --> 00:03:08,076
That's what happened after the peak: from January 2023 to September 2024, the yearly
rate fell from 6.4 percent to 2.4, while the price level itself rose about 5.4 percent.

23
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:14,628
So inflation is not gone, and it is not at its peak.
Which leaves the next question: what is pushing it up?

24
00:03:16,133 --> 00:03:24,917
The rate had been easing into the new year; then, from
March to May, it climbed to 3.3, 3.8 and 4.2 percent.

25
00:03:24,933 --> 00:03:33,717
NBC News said the economy had been "heading towards that number earlier
this year", the Fed's target, "but then with the Iran war prices shot up".

26
00:03:33,733 --> 00:03:37,669
The price tables confirm the jump,
but they don't say what caused it.

27
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:51,584
One line in them stands out, though: gasoline's own index leapt 24.9 percent in March alone and kept rising
through May before falling back in June, but the tables don't say how much of the overall jump that explains.

28
00:03:51,600 --> 00:04:01,152
September's report is more specific: gasoline rose 9.1 percent in
that one month and accounted for over half of the month's whole rise.

29
00:04:01,167 --> 00:04:09,255
But an economist on PBS NewsHour pointed out that "the war in
Iran is not causing medical care, dental care, toys, apparel".

30
00:04:09,267 --> 00:04:17,571
The report agrees that it isn't only fuel: used cars, airline
fares, medical care and apparel all went up in September.

31
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:28,784
But the spread is modest: prices outside food and energy, called core, rose 0.3
percent on the month, no faster than in August, and 2.6 percent over the year.

32
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:37,728
Some economists think the underlying rate is higher. "I think
underlying inflation is around 3%", Michael Strain said in June.

33
00:04:37,733 --> 00:04:48,221
Core has run a little under that all year, between 2.4 and 2.9 percent, and
it had slowed from 3.1 percent in the summer of 2025 before ticking back up.

34
00:04:48,533 --> 00:04:54,293
Housing runs hotter: the shelter
index rose 3.2 percent over the year.

35
00:04:54,300 --> 00:05:04,812
And tariffs? Economists at the New York Fed estimate they added around 2.9
percentage points to price growth for the goods they studied, by February 2026.

36
00:05:04,833 --> 00:05:14,097
They caution that it measures those goods against less-exposed ones, so the number is
illustrative, and it doesn't tell us how much of overall inflation tariffs explain.

37
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:21,492
So in September the biggest single push was gasoline, while
core sat a little under Strain's figure, with housing above it.

38
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,288
The people quoted here mostly weren't arguing about
the numbers; they were arguing about the yardstick.

39
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:35,876
So when someone tells you inflation is high or low,
ask: compared with what, and over which months?

40
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:42,260
And when you hear the rate is falling, check core, and remember
that a slower rate still means prices that are climbing.
